Explanation The user types www.foobar.com into their browser.
DNS translates www.foobar.com to the server's IP address (8.8.8.8).
The browser sends an HTTP request to the server.
Nginx (web server) receives the request and forwards it to the application server.
The application server processes the request, possibly interacting with the MySQL database.
The application server generates the response (HTML web page) and sends it back to Nginx.
Nginx sends the response to the user's browser.
The browser displays the web page.

1. Server: Physical or virtual machine hosting all components.

2. Domain name System: A representation of the server's IP address.

3. DNS 'www' record: Type A record that maps the 'www' subdomain to the server's IP address.

4. Web server (Nginx): Handles incoming HTTP requests and serves static files (HTML, CSS, images).

5. Application Server: Runs the web application code (PHP, Python, etc.) to generate dynamic content.

6. Database (MySQL): Stores the application data (users, articles, etc.).

7. Communication: The server uses TCP/IP to communicate with the user's computer.

Issues with this infrastructure
SPOF (Single Point of Failure): If the server goes down, the website is inaccessible.
Maintenance and Deployment: Any maintenance or deployment that requires a server restart will result in a service interruption.
Scalability: The infrastructure cannot easily handle a sudden increase in traffic.